# LDBC SNB -- value_sorted_date schema: the type_scoped_date design (Post/Comment creationDate index
# scoped per type), PLUS the same two indexes stored VALUE-sorted by date (dim-2). The full
# nebula-equivalent global top-k. Pick with `--schema value_sorted_date`.
#
# WHY. type_scoped_date (dim-1) keys the message-date index per type, so a "newest messages" top-k
# walks it candidate-free -- a BYTE win (no cross-type candidate set). But the day-token posting still
# stores its uids in UID order, so the top-k must GET the exact ms of every same-day candidate to rank
# them: NO ops win. dim-2 fixes that. `value_sorted` stores each day bucket as DATE-descending
# [vid, ms] pairs, so the walk reads each touched day's value already in date order and ranks with no
# per-candidate ms GET -- the ops win, matching nebula's ms-in-key index. The stored ms also serves the
# result's creationDate and the exact intra-day maxDate filter, both GET-free.
#
# COMPOSITION. value_sorted only orders the VALUE; it relies on type_scoped for the per-type KEY (a
# predicate-global bucket is shared by every dated type, so value-sorting it would mix Post's [vid, ms]
# pairs with Person's plain vids). So we include type_scoped_date and add value_sorted on top -- the two
# knobs compose (dim-1 = per-type key -> bytes; dim-2 = date-ordered value -> ops). Person/Forum
# creationDate stay canonical (predicate-global, uid-ordered). Everything else matches canonical.

include type_scoped_date

# Add value_sorted to the two message-date indexes. `include type_scoped_date` already declares them as
# per-type sorted date indexes; the trailing `value_sorted` flips only the VALUE ordering (the index
# still exists, still sorted, still per-type). type_scoped is re-stated so this line reads on its own and
# satisfies the value_sorted -> type_scoped invariant even when read in isolation.
index Post.creationDate type_scoped value_sorted
index Comment.creationDate type_scoped value_sorted
